/*! @arch_page arch-disagg-ingest-stable-contracts Stable and ingest table contracts

Contract: Stable and Ingest Tables

See also @ref arch-disagg-layered for the high-level architecture of layered tables.

This page defines the relationship between the ingest and stable tables used in WiredTiger’s layered
tables, and captures the invariants required for correctness.

Contract entries follow this format:

- Rule: the expected invariant.
- Rationale: why this rule exists.
- Violation Consequence: what goes wrong if the rule is violated.
- [XX.NN]: reference to relevant implementation details.

@section layered-contract-background Background on layered tables

Layered tables are a new access method in WiredTiger designed to support distributed systems that
leverage shared storage for the majority of a table’s data. This mechanism enables a single node to
perform writes to the table, while other nodes maintain a consistent view by tracking recent changes.

Having nodes follow along with recent changes allows two key features:
- Follower nodes can serve read operations on recent data without needing to access the shared storage
  directly.
- Follower nodes can quickly transition to take over write responsibilities if the current leader
  (writer) becomes unavailable.

These semantics are achieved by using two underlying constituents: a shared stable table and an ingest
table. The node responsible for writing to shared storage operates directly on a single shared table and
has the guarantee that no other node will perform writes to that table. All other nodes will also have an
ingest table, which holds a short-term view of recent changes. This view is layered over the shared stable
table, so any operations on a node with both an ingest and stable table will first look for a result in
the ingest table, and if no result is found, fall back on the shared table.

Layered tables are primarily for Disaggregated Storage Clusters (DSC) but can also be used outside of DSC
for testing purposes, in particular, via WiredTiger’s local Page and Log Mock (PALM) infrastructure.

Stable Table:
- A table shared across nodes in the cluster
- Backed by a block manager that uses shared storage and supports having multiple different WiredTiger
  nodes share the same underlying data structure

Ingest Table:
- A short-term in-memory table that stores data that has been changed more recently than is available in
  the stable table
- Supersedes checkpointed content in the stable table
- This table is specific to a particular node

Leader (Primary):
- All operations are served directly from the stable table
- Ingest table is always empty
- Only the leader can create new checkpoints

Follower (Standby):
- Stable table contains read-only checkpointed data from the primary
- Ingest table contains all changes applied from oplog since the last checkpoint
- When a new checkpoint is fetched, ingest table is cleaned up (GCed)

@section layered-contract-overview Overview

The contracts in this document are grouped into:

- Timestamp rules
- Leader-follower transition rules
- Table creation and schema rules
- Checkpoint rules
- Insertion and visibility rules
- Garbage collection rules
- Durability and consistency considerations

Rule blocks use the following structure:
- Rule
- Rationale
- Violation Consequence
- Optional implementation references, e.g. [TS.01]

@section layered-contract-timestamps Timestamp rules

@subsection layered-contract-ts-ordering Timestamp ordering

Rule: For any key K, if K appears in both the ingest table with timestamp T1 and in the stable table
with timestamp T2, then T1 >= T2. Ingest must never hold updates that are older than the corresponding
updates in stable. Duplicate keys across ingest and stable are only valid if the ingest version is newer
or equal to the stable table version.

"If we have a K,V at timestamp T1 in the ingest table, and the same key is available at timestamp T2 in
the stable table, I never expect T1 < T2."

Rationale:
On the follower, the stable table stores checkpointed, durable data retrieved from the Shared Log Service
(SLS). The ingest table handles newer, non-checkpointed updates. This ordering ensures:
- Ingest always contains the most recent visible version for any key
- Duplicate keys are permitted when ingest holds a newer version
- Oplog application can safely insert into ingest while stable retains the previous version until checkpoint

Movement from ingest to stable occurs during the draining process, when a follower steps up to be leader.
During draining, only ingest entries with timestamps newer than the last checkpoint timestamp are moved to
stable. In all other cases, the stable table receives newer content through checkpoint pickup from SLS (not
by copying from ingest tables).

This ensures that the stable table always reflects the latest persisted state, and ingest holds the more
recent data.

Violation Consequence:
If ingest contains an update with a timestamp older than what is in stable:
- It violates the logic that ingest timestamps are newer and may cause reconciliation to fail or silently
  corrupt stable state.
- Pruning logic filters ingest entries based on checkpoint timestamps; older-than-stable entries may be
  incorrectly discarded.
- Layered cursors merge views across ingest and stable. If timestamp order is reversed, reads may return
  stale or conflicting data.

@subsection layered-contract-ts-configs Timestamp configs (logging disabled)

Rule: Both ingest and stable tables must be created with write-ahead-logging disabled
(log=(enabled=false)) [TS.01].

Rationale:
WiredTiger skips assigning timestamps on updates when logging is enabled, as it treats durability as
guaranteed by the write-ahead log. Layered tables rely on timestamps for garbage collection and
reconciliation. Layered table constituents must retain updates with timestamps until reconciliation and GC.

Violation Consequence:
If logging is enabled:
- Updates will be missing timestamps.
- GC interprets these as outdated and aggressively tombstones them.
- This leads to missing records on scan, partial query results, and incorrect reconciliation.

@section layered-contract-leader-follower Leader-follower transition rules

Leader state:
- Empty ingest table
- Populated shared stable table

Follower state:
- Populated in-memory ingest table with most recent operations
- Read-only checkpointed stable table

The ingest table contains all updates since the last checkpoint, but it is not required to immediately
discard content older than that point. Older entries can remain until GCed. Followers have their own
in-memory instance of the stable table. All instances are backed by the same persisted data in shared
storage (i.e. the page service).

@subsection layered-contract-step-up-drain Step-up draining: ingest must be drained

Rule: When a follower node is promoted to leader, it must transfer all qualifying updates from its in-memory
ingest table to the stable table. Only updates with timestamps newer than the last checkpoint can be
transferred during this draining process [SU.01].

Rationale:
During step-up, the new leader becomes responsible for durability. It must move any post-checkpoint updates
from the ingest table into the stable table before enabling checkpoints. Draining is done under internal
sessions and locking, as it requires that no other concurrent operations are happening.

Violation Consequence:
If the ingest table is not drained:
- Checkpointing will ignore recent updates, resulting in lost data across restarts or rollbacks.
- Future reconciliation attempts could misinterpret ingest content as conflicting or outdated, leading to stale
  updates being persisted or served to readers.

@subsection layered-contract-step-up-empty-ingest Step-up states: ingest should always be empty on the leader

Rule: An ingest table is always empty on the leader node. After step-up and a successful ingest drain, the
new leader's ingest table must be empty [SU.03].

Rationale:
The ingest table holds recent, un-checkpointed updates. When transitioning from follower to leader, it migrates
all post-checkpoint ingest content into the stable table. Once drained, ingest should be cleared to avoid
reprocessing or retaining stale data. A clean ingest table allows the leader to begin fresh oplog application
and new writes.

Violation Consequence:
If ingest still contains drained updates after promotion:
- The leader may re-apply or re-prune those entries incorrectly.
- This causes duplicate data, timestamp inconsistencies, and incorrect checkpoint content.

@subsection layered-contract-step-up-checkpoint Step-up checkpoints: new leader must pick up the latest complete checkpoint

Rule: When a follower steps up to be leader, it must pick up the most recent complete checkpoint from shared
storage before accepting writes. This ensures the leader begins from the same state as the previous leader.

Followers are generally expected to stay close to recent checkpoints, but they are not required to pick up
the latest one as followers. A follower can operate on an older checkpoint and rely on its ingest table to
layer in more recent updates until reconfiguration advances its state.

A checkpoint is considered complete once dirty data in the stable table has been reconciled, the stable table
is flushed to the page/log service, and the corresponding metadata entry is persisted to the shared metadata
file.

Rationale:
The new leader must start from the same durable state that all nodes can reference. Using the latest complete
checkpoint ensures the new leader is aligned with the previous leader’s final committed state.

Violation Consequence:
If a new leader does not pick up the latest complete checkpoint:
- It risks diverging and losing data, breaking replication correctness and corrupting recovery.
- Failing to pick up the latest checkpoint results in a shutdown.

@subsection layered-contract-role-isolation Role transition isolation

Rule: During step-up or step-down, no concurrent user operations can run on layered tables. This requires all
layered cursors to be closed and any active transactions to be aborted before the state transition begins.
Multiple ingest tables can be drained concurrently, and writes to disk through I/O operations (such as
eviction or checkpointing) can still occur in parallel. Any other threads calling reconfigure are blocked
until the transition is complete [SU.02].

Rationale:
Role transitions modify layered table state (e.g. draining ingest into stable during step-up [SU.01], stopping
writes during step-down [SD.02]). To ensure we transition correctly, user operations must be isolated from
these transitions. Cursor closure guarantees no conflicting read/write operations, while exclusivity ensures
updates are applied consistently and in order.

Violation Consequence:
Step-up:
- If user operations overlap ingest draining, updates can be duplicated, skipped, or applied out of order.
- This breaks the layered table’s timestamp ordering invariant and can lead to reconciliation errors or
  corrupted stable state.

Step-down:
- If user operations continue after step-down, they may attempt writes after losing leader status, triggering
  forced rollbacks and potential corruption on layered tables.

@subsection layered-contract-step-down-queue Step-down abandoning checkpoints: clearing the metadata queue

Rule: When a leader steps down to a follower, it must clear its metadata copy queue as part of abandoning
checkpoint responsibilities [SD.01].

Rationale:
The metadata copy queue tracks metadata entries that need to be written to the shared metadata store during
the next checkpoint. Once a leader steps down, it no longer has authority to complete checkpoint operations.

Violation Consequence:
If the queue is not cleared on step-down:
- Stale metadata may persist and be applied incorrectly during future checkpoints.
- This leads to inconsistent shared metadata and potential data corruption in replication and recovery
  processes.

@section layered-contract-schema Table creation and schema rules

@subsection layered-contract-schema-ingest-required Schema requirement: ingest table presence on followers

Rule: For follower nodes, ingest tables are strictly required when opening a layered table. If the ingest table
does not exist in the local metadata store, it must be created immediately. This applies when picking up a
layered table normally and when discovering a new layered table through shared metadata, through:
- Checkpoint pickup: startup or recovery when layered table metadata appears for the first time
- Oplog application: when a follower encounters schema operations (e.g. table creation or deletion) when the
  oplog is applied to its ingest table

Leader nodes may omit ingest tables, but if an ingest table is present, it must be valid [MT.01]. Missing ingest
tables on leaders are eventually created.

Rationale:
Follower nodes depend on ingest tables to contain recent, non-checkpointed updates that have not yet been
reflected in the stable table. Leader nodes write directly to the stable table. While stable tables do not
strictly require ingest table presence prior to opening a layered table, missing ingest tables on leaders
complicate role transitions (during step-down or restart) [MT.02].

Violation Consequence:
If the ingest table is missing:
- Follower nodes cannot apply the oplog or serve reads with recent updates.

@subsection layered-contract-schema-stable-required Missing tables: layered tables must have a stable constituent

Rule: Layered tables should always have a stable table constituent. Transient exceptions exist but should be
treated as temporary states only. Whenever possible, create operations should clean up after themselves to
avoid leaving behind incomplete layered tables.

Rationale:
The stable table stores the durable, checkpointed state of a layered table. While a follower may temporarily
operate with only the ingest table (e.g. after applying a create from the oplog), a complete layered table
must eventually include a stable constituent. Since followers cannot create stable tables, the stable
constituent is created either by the leader during checkpointing or by the node itself during step-up.
Enforcing the presence of a stable table ensures follower nodes can transition to leader without additional
setup [MT.03].

A layered table may exist without a stable table temporarily in these scenarios:
- Oplog application:
  - Follower applying a create operation from the oplog. The follower creates only the ingest table, since only
    leaders can create stable tables. The follower does not see the stable constituent until it picks up a
    checkpoint from the leader.
- Leader crash:
  - If the leader crashes after the create operation but before checkpointing, a follower may step up holding
    only an ingest table. The step-up process resolves this by creating the missing stable table [MT.04].
- Partial create failure:
  - If a layered table create fails partway, metadata may be left with only the ingest constituent. This should
    be treated as a temporary, incomplete state and cleaned up where possible.

Violation Consequence:
If a promoted node does not create stable tables:
- It cannot generate checkpoints. This breaks replication durability and causes errors during checkpointing
  or backup.
- Role transitions (step-up) may fail or require expensive table creation.

@subsection layered-contract-schema-namespaces Schema namespaces

Rule: Ingest and stable tables must be assigned the correct namespace based on their btree ID [SR.01].

Rationale:
Namespaces ensure that leader nodes can allocate IDs for shared btrees independently, without risk of clashing
with IDs allocated for ingest (or other local) tables on different nodes. This separation prevents conflicts
between globally visible stable tables and node-local ingest tables, while still allowing layered cursors,
schema operations, and the layered table manager to operate correctly.

Violation Consequence:
If namespaces are assigned incorrectly:
- A shared btree could end up with the same ID as an ingest table on another node.
- Ingest tables could be treated as shared, or stable tables as local.
- This can break checkpointing, oplog application, role transitions, and schema operations.

@section layered-contract-checkpoint Checkpoint rules

For all checkpoints Ci and Cj, if Ci comes before Cj, its timestamp must be earlier or equal.

@subsection layered-contract-ckpt-complete Checkpoint completeness

Rule: A checkpoint is considered complete when:
- Dirty data in the stable table is reconciled
- The stable table has been flushed to the page/log service
- The local metadata entry has been stored in the shared metadata file

Checkpoints are designed to be atomic [CP.01].

Rationale:
The stable table contains the durable, checkpointed state of the layered table, and the shared metadata file
records the latest checkpoint information. The durability of the checkpoint does not depend on local tables
(e.g. WiredTiger.wt or metadata) being flushed to disk, as per the DSC model where local files are treated as
disposable.

"If there were N tables updated in the checkpoint, note that the primary must have updated N entries in its
metadata during the checkpoint. So the standby does as well. There's no additional burden on the standby
implied."

Violation Consequence:
If we wait on local files to define when a checkpoint is complete:
- It could give a false sense of durability, since those files might not survive a crash.
- Recovery on secondaries may break if shared table state and checkpoint metadata are out of sync.
- It prevents optimizations like "quick checkpoints", which skip local fsync.

@subsection layered-contract-ckpt-ingest Ingest table and checkpoints

Rule: The ingest table does not get checkpointed and is not persisted across restarts. Content in the ingest
table that has not been drained to the stable table will be lost on restart [CP.02].

Rationale:
The ingest table is a short-term, in-memory table. Its role is to buffer updates not yet in the stable table
and serve reads with the most recent updates.

Violation Consequence:
Persisting the ingest table or checkpointing it:
- Violates the core architecture of layered tables, which separates short-term and durable data.
- Breaks checkpoint semantics that assume ingest tables are drained and cleared.
- Detrimentally impacts leader-follower syncs and recovery.

@section layered-contract-visibility Insertion and visibility rules

@subsection layered-contract-dup-identical Duplicates: same-version pairs must be bitwise identical

Rule: At any given timestamp, WiredTiger must only return one visible version of a key. If the same version
exists in both ingest and stable tables, they must be bitwise identical [DP.01].

Rationale:
The layered cursor merges results from the ingest and stable cursors. If both point to the same key-version,
they must agree exactly, or we risk inconsistent read behavior.

Violation Consequence:
If the versions differ:
- Snapshot isolation may break.
- Readers may see inconsistent or invalid data.
- Causes correctness issues in replication and recovery.

@subsection layered-contract-dup-insert Duplicates: insert ops must check both ingest and stable tables

Rule: When inserting a key with overwrite=false, we must check for duplicates in both the ingest and stable
tables [DP.02].

Rationale:
If the same key exists in either ingest or stable, and the insert is not allowed to overwrite, then the insert
operation must not be allowed. This is to ensure there are no conflicting versions of the same key.

Violation Consequence:
If only one table is checked:
- Duplicate keys may be inserted.
- Leads to multiple versions at the same timestamp.
- Breaks expected uniqueness guarantees and could corrupt internal data structures.

@subsection layered-contract-read-prefer-ingest Duplicates: reads prefer ingest cursor

Rule: If both the ingest and stable cursors are positioned on the same key, the ingest cursor is selected when
returning results to the application [DP.03].

Rationale:
During cursor traversal, the layered cursor tracks overlapping positions to ensure that the ingest cursor is
chosen to return results. This respects the invariant that ingest holds the more recent version.

Violation Consequence:
Returning the stable cursor instead:
- Exposes outdated data.
- Violates timestamp precedence.
- Breaks expectations for correct read ordering and could impact correctness in transactional reads.

@section layered-contract-gc Garbage collection rules

@subsection layered-contract-gc-ingest-only GC: only the ingest table is garbage collected

Rule: GC applies to the ingest table only. Once data is available in the stable table, it is no longer needed
by active readers, and the corresponding ingest entries must be removed [GC.01]. GC is achieved through
reconciliation by compacting pages (dropping obsolete entries) and re-instantiating the new page image in
memory. Eviction is then able to reclaim the cache space. The stable table is not GCed.

Rationale:
Think of the ingest table as a short-lived FIFO buffer layered over the stable table. The newest updates land
in the ingest table, and once those updates are persisted into the stable table through a checkpoint pickup
from shared storage, ingest must discard the older copies. This prevents memory from ballooning under
continuous oplog application and ensures the ingest stays within its cache limits.

GC happens naturally during reconciliation:
- Ingest pages are compacted.
- Obsolete entries are removed.
- The new page is written in memory (overwriting the previous image).

Stable tables do not accumulate unbounded amounts of data. Each stable table has its own cache limit. If the
cache does not contain the page needed, it is retrieved from the shared storage service.

Violation Consequence:
If ingest GC fails to remove obsolete data:
- Ingest will grow unbounded, filling cache and blocking forward progress.
- Reads may be slowed by deep version chains.
- Role transitions become inconsistent due to stale data lingering in the ingest table.

@section layered-contract-durability Durability and consistency

In DSC, durability is provided by checkpoints, unlike classic WiredTiger where durability is provided by the
write-ahead-log (WAL).

Stable table checkpoints:
- Once we’ve checkpointed, shared storage is used to persist data.

Oplog replay:
- Since DSC does not run RTS (due to recovery time objectives), and since the oplog is now a layered table,
  MongoDB can use the layered table to replay operations.

WT_SESSION::checkpoint guarantees the data is durable. However, other nodes will not see the checkpoint until
they pick it up (visibility is coordinated at the replication layer, not by WiredTiger).

To maintain replica consistency:
- All nodes use the same URI for each layered table.
- For the same key, all nodes store the same value and timestamp (identical byte-wise) in the stable table.
- Updates to the all Durable timestamp result in all replicas generating the same content (keys and values).

Further reading at the Layered Table API contract.

@section layered-contract-questions Questions

@subsection layered-contract-q-stable-shared Q: Is the stable table the shared table? Why?

A: A shared table is a table that is backed by a block manager that uses shared storage, and supports having
multiple different WiredTiger nodes share the same underlying data structure. It will be common that the
stable constituent in a layered table will be a shared table.

@subsection layered-contract-q-layered-scope Q: Is a layered table shared across the cluster? Or is a layered table associated with only one node?

A: The stable portion of the layered table is shared, and the metadata entry for the layered table is present on
all nodes. The ingest portion of the layered table is specific to a particular node.

@subsection layered-contract-q-manager Q: What is the role of WT_LAYERED_TABLE_MANAGER?

A: There is no usage for WT_LAYERED_TABLE_MANAGER, it is just a data structure holding all the opened layered
tables.

The entries stored in the manager struct correspond to layered tables and refer to open layered tables.

@subsection layered-contract-q-stable-gc Q: Is the stable table garbage collected?

A: No, stable tables are not GCed. Stable tables do not accumulate unbounded amounts of data. Each stable table
has its own cache limit. If the cache does not contain the page needed, it is retrieved from the shared
storage service.

@section layered-contract-impl-references References to WT implementation

@subsection layered-contract-impl-ts Timestamps

[TS.01]
During layered table creation in schema_create.c, the ingest and stable tables are created with logging
explicitly disabled via log=(enabled=false). This is to ensure timestamps are retained for updates.

@subsection layered-contract-impl-leader Leader-follower transitions

[SU.01]
Draining logic is implemented in conn_layered.c. During step-up in __layered_drain_ingest_tables, WiredTiger
iterates through all layered tables and calls __layered_copy_ingest_table on them, filtering for entries with
durable_start_ts > last_checkpoint_timestamp.

During the step-up process, WiredTiger acquires a spinlock on WT_LAYERED_TABLE_MANAGER to prevent concurrent
user operations. Draining is performed under internal sessions to guarantee isolation.

[SU.02]
Since all reconfigurations are serialized (via reconfig_lock and the WT_CONN_RECONFIGURING flag — a general
WiredTiger rule, not specific to layered tables), role transitions (step-up/step-down) inherit this
serialization because they are performed through the reconfig path. As a result, other reconfiguration attempts
are blocked until the transition completes.

[SU.03]
Layered table architecture is not yet set in stone. One open consideration is whether to delete the ingest
table after draining, in addition to clearing its contents. A deleted ingest table is by definition empty and
would satisfy the empty-ingest-on-leader rule.

[SD.01]
The copy_metadata_qh is a queue of metadata entries awaiting copy from the local metadata table to the shared
metadata table in DSC. On leader step-down, queue clearing is performed by __disagg_copy_metadata_clear and
triggered by __wti_disagg_conn_config.

[SD.02]
On step-down, the system checks update and session->txn->mod_count to detect uncommitted changes, issuing a
WT_ROLLBACK to prevent writes after the leader steps down. Cursor state is then reset through
__clayered_adjust_state, which reopens cursors as needed. This reopening logic also considers read timestamps
and snapshots to ensure cursors match the node’s new role.

@subsection layered-contract-impl-schema Table creation and schema rules

[MT.01]
The ingest table is created either when a checkpoint is picked up or on the first open after a restart.
__schema_open_layered_ingest opens the ingest table and extracts the btree_id to store in the WT_LAYERED_TABLE
structure.

[MT.02]
During role transitions for a leader node, such as step-down or restart followed by role reassignment, the
newly demoted or restarted node would need to dynamically create ingest tables for all layered tables it
manages if they do not already exist, which can be prohibitively expensive, especially under memory pressure
or with many layered tables. Maintaining ingest tables proactively avoids this cost and ensures smoother
transitions.

[MT.03]
If a follower creates a layered table from an oplog entry, only the ingest table is created (since only the
leader can create a stable table). If the leader crashes before checkpointing, and the follower steps up, it
must call __layered_create_missing_stable_tables to ensure a stable table exists.

[MT.04]
Creation and recovery of missing components is handled by:
- __layered_create_missing_ingest_table – creates an ingest table when it does not exist locally.
- __layered_create_missing_stable_table – creates a stable table when needed, such as during step-up or
  checkpoint application.

Potential future design:
- There is consideration being put to creating ingest tables on demand and dropping them when they are no
  longer needed. This would reduce the memory and management overhead of maintaining ingest tables for layered
  tables that are not actively receiving updates (see WT-15176).

[SR.01]
The namespace is encoded in the last few bits of the btree ID using the namespace manipulation macros.
Ingest tables must have namespace 0 (non-shared), and stable tables must have namespace 1 (shared).

@subsection layered-contract-impl-ckpt Checkpoint

[CP.01]
In DSC, local files are treated as disposable. This includes files like WiredTiger.wt, metadata tables, and any
local-only content. On crash or restart, they are rebuilt from WiredTigerShared.wt and the stable tables.

[CP.02]
Ingest tables are created with in_memory=true and log=(enabled=false), so they are only in memory and excluded
from write-ahead-logging.

@subsection layered-contract-impl-visibility Insertion and visibility

[DP.01]
The WT_CLAYERED_MULTIPLE flag tracks when cursors have values for the same key-version, ensuring that only one
version is returned (conn_layered.c).

[DP.02]
WiredTiger has duplicate key detection across both the ingest and stable tables. In __clayered_insert, without
the overwrite flag (overwrite=false), __clayered_lookup searches both ingest and stable, and returns
WT_DUPLICATE_KEY if the key is in either table.

[DP.03]
The layered cursor tracks overlapping positions using WT_CLAYERED_MULTIPLE.

@subsection layered-contract-impl-gc Garbage collection

[GC.01]
Ingest entries are garbage collected once their durable timestamp is less than or equal to the prune timestamp,
meaning they have been included in a checkpoint with no active cursors depending on them. This guarantee is
enforced by __layered_update_gc_ingest_tables_prune_timestamps, which advances prune timestamps during
checkpoint pickup and ensures they are monotonically increasing. This mechanism safely discards obsolete ingest
updates after the stable table has persisted them.

@section layered-contract-glossary Glossary

Stable table:
- A shared table containing checkpointed, durable data, that is shared cluster-wide. Only the leader is allowed
  to write to it, with followers only granted read privileges.

Ingest table:
- A short-term, node-local table that stores recent, non-checkpointed updates. It is layered over the stable
  table so reads check it first for the most recent data before falling back to the stable table.

Layered table creation:
- The act of defining the table (including its constituent ingest and stable tables) in metadata, typically
  during checkpoint pickup or schema application.

@section layered-contract-resources Relevant resources

- Storage Engines work for Disaggregated Storage
- Layered Table API contract
- Dropping Layered Tables
- Engineering Proposal – Disaggregated Storage

*/
